          "create_time": 1793814610.642,
          "update_time": 1793814627.355,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "The plan is to turn the first next-order comparison into a **quantitative induction in determinant order**. The central difficulty is proving that the approximation error stays below the shrinking determinant margin as both order \\(r\\) and index \\(k\\) increase.\n\n1. **Verify the first next-order case.**  \n   Sol’s fixed task is to check the saved inputs for \\((r,k)=(4,1)\\). The preparatory lemma gives\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R_{4,1}/q^*_{4,1}<1/15,\n   \\qquad Q_{4,1}>3\\times10^{-31},\n   \\]\n   conditional on those checks. No new integration is needed. This would establish one successful step, while leaving the general extension open.\n\n2. **Resolve the different index regimes at order four.**  \n   We need justified margins for the remaining small indices \\(k=2,3\\), the boundary transition \\(k=4\\), and the pure-volume regime beginning at \\(k=5\\). Existing data cover the inputs for \\(k=2,3\\); \\(k=4,5\\) require additional moments. Any acquisition should follow a bounded analytical specification. Checking these representatives would still leave the unbounded index range to prove.\n\n3. **Develop estimates that remain useful at large indices.**  \n   [The preparatory analysis](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/07_positive_theta_primitives/01_jensen/01_mellin_curvature/10_radial_green_determinants/notes/05_RGD_B03_PREPARATORY_ORDER_DEPENDENCE_20261104.md) proves that our present entrywise relative-error bound eventually becomes large. The next conceptual question is whether sharper moment-tail estimates and the cancellations in the combined determinant polynomial overcome that loss. A possible route is a finite small-index region followed by an analytical large-index estimate. Its feasibility remains unresolved.\n\n4. **Prove a quantitative induction lemma.**  \n   For each claimed domain, we need both\n   \\[\n   q^*_{r,k}=D^*_{r+1,k}D^*_{r-1,k}>0,\n   \\qquad\n   \\mathcal R_{r,k}\\le \\rho_{r,k}q^*_{r,k},\n   \\quad \\rho_{r,k}<1.\n   \\]\n   The reference margin must be proved independently. With actual lower-order positivity established, the determinant identity then transfers positivity to order \\(r+1\\). The lemma must control polynomial degree, boundary contributions and cancellation losses explicitly.\n\n5. **Extend only over the domain those estimates justify.**  \n   A finite-order extension would be useful method development. Arbitrary-order extension requires bounds that continue to close as \\(r\\) grows. If the reference margin fails or the error consumes it, that identifies a specific repair-or-stop decision for B02.\n\nOnly the first step currently has a frozen execution contract. **Full B03 remains gated.**\n\n**Next: [E] RGD-E02 r2 — Sol**, following [Packet08](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/07_positive_theta_primitives/01_jensen/01_mellin_curvature/10_radial_green_determinants/research-progress/handoffs/08_RGD_E02_R2_EXISTING_DATA_NEXT_ORDER_AND_MODEL_CHANGE_20261104.md). The subsequent unresolved margin and uniformity questions belong to **[B] RGD-B03 — Astra**."
            ]
